# INC-003: Cron Persistence Detection

## What this is

After an attacker gets in (INC-001) and becomes root (INC-002), they need a way back. A cron job is one of the simplest: "every minute, run this script as root." Wazuh's default File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) technically watches the folder where cron jobs live, but it is configured so loosely that a planted cron job goes unnoticed for up to 12 hours, and when it finally alerts, nothing says "persistence". I found both gaps, fixed them, and tested with a harmless cron job.

## The gaps

**1. Timing.** FIM scans on a schedule, not in real time:

```bash
sudo sed -n '/<syscheck>/,/<\/syscheck>/p' /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
```

```xml
<frequency>43200</frequency>
<directories>/etc,/usr/bin,/usr/sbin</directories>
```

`43200` seconds is 12 hours, and there is no `realtime="yes"` anywhere. `/etc/cron.d` is covered, but a new file there can sit unnoticed for half a day. My first test file (`soc-test`) never produced a FIM alert at all.

**2. Meaning.** Once real-time was on, the default alert for a new cron file was rule 554: level 5, "File added to the system", no MITRE mapping. The same rule fires for any new file in `/etc`, so it looks like noise. Nothing says this is a root job running every minute.

**Also noted:**
- **Baseline blind spot:** a file planted before FIM was tuned gets absorbed into the baseline and never alerts as "added". The original `soc-test` showed up later only as a deletion.
- **Attribution:** FIM shows the owner as `root` because the file was written via `sudo`. Only the separate sudo alert (rule 5402) named the user, `pvee`.

## The fix

**1. Real-time monitoring on the cron paths**, added to `/var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf` inside `<syscheck>`:

```xml
<directories realtime="yes" check_all="yes" report_changes="yes">/etc/cron.d,/etc/cron.hourly,/etc/cron.daily,/etc/cron.weekly,/etc/crontab,/var/spool/cron</directories>
```

**2. A custom rule**, added to `/var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml`:

```xml
<group name="syscheck,persistence,">
  <rule id="100301" level="12">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">^/etc/cron\.d/|^/etc/cron\.(hourly|daily|weekly|monthly)/|^/etc/crontab|^/var/spool/cron/</field>
    <description>Persistence: new cron job file created ($(file)).</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,</group>
  </rule>
</group>
```

100301 — a new file in any cron location → level 12 alert tagged as persistence.

Load it (check the rules parse first, because a bad rule file stops the manager from starting):

```bash
sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager
sudo systemctl status wazuh-manager
```

**Lesson learned:** my first version of the rule used a regex group without `type="pcre2"`. Wazuh's default regex engine doesn't support that syntax, so the rule failed to load and the whole manager refused to start. Always run `wazuh-analysisd -t` before restarting.

## Proof it works

Attack simulation: a harmless root cron job that only appends a timestamp to a file:

```bash
echo '* * * * * root echo "soc-lab persistence test $(date)" >> /tmp/soc-test.log' | sudo tee /etc/cron.d/soc-test4
```

Check the result:

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/ossec/logs/alerts/alerts.json | jq -r 'select(.syscheck.path != null) | [.timestamp[11:19], .rule.id, .rule.level, .rule.description, (.rule.mitre.id // [] | join(",")), .syscheck.path] | @tsv'
```

What you'll see:

| Time | Rule | Level | What happened |
|---|---|---|---|
| 19:00:59 | 554 | 5 | `soc-test2` created, real-time on, default rule: generic "file added", no MITRE |
| 19:08:53 | **100301** | **12** | `soc-test4` created, custom rule: "Persistence: new cron job file created", **T1053.003** |
| 19:09:47 | 553 | 7 | Cleanup: four "File deleted" alerts |

The FIM record matched the filesystem: the SHA-256 in the alert (`93756fa0…c4435`) was identical to `sha256sum` on the file, as were the size, permissions, owner and inode. The hash is a usable indicator for threat-intel lookups or for searching other hosts.

