## Limitations

- **Only new files are covered.** Rule 100301 chains off rule 554 ("added"). Editing an existing cron file triggers rule 550 instead, which the rule doesn't cover.
- **Baseline blind spot.** Persistence planted before FIM was tuned is treated as normal.
- **No automatic attribution.** `whodata="yes"` (needs `auditd`) would record which user and process made the change.
- **Deletions are low severity.** Rule 553 fires at level 7 even on cron paths, but removing a job can also be an attacker covering their tracks.
- **Other persistence is not covered.** Systemd services and `authorized_keys` are separate techniques, which are the subject of INC-004 and beyond.

## What I'd do next in a real environment

- Read the cron file and identify what it runs (look for `curl`, `wget`, `base64`, or reverse shells)
- Identify who created it and the session they came from
- Remove the job and check for other persistence (systemd units, `authorized_keys`, shell profiles)
- Hash the file and search other hosts for the same indicator
- Rotate credentials and review everything the account did after the initial access

